Rem ==================================================================
Rem ephemeris.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: the sky as arithmetic
Rem
Rem Where everything is, for any observer and any date: sidereal time,
Rem the transform from a sky position to an altitude and a compass
Rem azimuth, the sun and the moon as sky positions, the moon's phase,
Rem age, distance, rise and set, and the five naked-eye planets from
Rem Keplerian elements. The machine geo.shoddy's out-of-scope promised.
Rem
Rem PURE THROUGHOUT, AND NO CLOCK ANYWHERE NEAR IT. Every word takes a
Rem Julian date and a UTC hour as arguments; julian.shoddy makes one
Rem from a calendar date (JulDayNumber, or geo's GeoJulian which is the
Rem same word by another name), and clock.shoddy's ClockDate supplies
Rem "today" at the caller's edge. Nothing here is a capability.
Rem
Rem THE BOUNDARY WITH GEO, STATED SO IT CANNOT DRIFT. geo answers the
Rem sun AS EXPERIENCED AT A PLACE — rise, set, noon, day length,
Rem twilight — and stays the canonical word for all of those. This
Rem machine answers the SKY AS OBSERVED FROM A PLACE: where any body
Rem is, in anyone's sky, at any instant. EphSun exists here because the
Rem moon's phase and every planet's geometry need the sun's position,
Rem and because "where is the sun in MY sky right now" is EphAltAz of
Rem it — a different question from geo's, spelled differently. The two
Rem machines' suns are two small private models of one star, and
Rem tst/ephemeris.shoddy asserts they agree within the joint stated
Rem accuracy, so the tolerated duplication is tested rather than
Rem trusted.
Rem
Rem Conventions, all of them load-bearing:
Rem
Rem   * DEGREES AT EVERY BOUNDARY, exactly as geo and for geo's stated
Rem     reasons. Right ascension is degrees 0..360, not hours — one
Rem     unit for every angle, and a reader who wants hours divides by
Rem     15 visibly. Declination and altitude are degrees either side
Rem     of zero; azimuth is geo's compass convention, 0..360, north 0,
Rem     clockwise, so a caller can point a compass at a planet.
Rem
Rem   * A SKY POSITION IS EphSky AND NOT GeoPt, DELIBERATELY, though
Rem     both live on a sphere. Declination is latitude-shaped and right
Rem     ascension longitude-shaped, but GeoWrapLon folds into
Rem     (-180, 180] and would mangle a right ascension — and a word
Rem     accepting either record would someday be handed the wrong one
Rem     silently. Two meanings, two types.
Rem
Rem   * METRES FOR THE ONE DISTANCE, as geo says metres. There is no
Rem     unit mode; eng converts at the edge.
Rem
Rem   * ACCURACY IS A NUMBER, PER BODY. The sun is the low-precision
Rem     model geo uses, good to about a minute of arc. The moon is the
Rem     Astronomical Almanac's low-precision series: about a third of a
Rem     degree in longitude, a fifth in latitude, GEOCENTRIC — the
Rem     parallax an observer on the surface adds (up to a degree) is
Rem     deliberately not modelled, and the rise altitude below folds
Rem     its average into the horizon instead, which is the almanac's
Rem     own convention for rise times. The planets are the JPL
Rem     approximate Keplerian elements, valid 1800..2050, worst case
Rem     well under a degree there. Rise and set times are good to a few
Rem     minutes, which is also what the moon's own motion makes of any
Rem     almanac's minute.
Rem
Rem   * OPTION IS THE DESIGN FOR RISE AND SET, exactly as it is in geo:
Rem     a circumpolar moon has no rise because it never set, and near a
Rem     pole that is an ordinary answer, not an error. THE SCAN IS THE
Rem     UTC DAY, 0..24: roughly once a month a calendar day genuinely
Rem     has no moonrise — the moon rises about fifty minutes later each
Rem     day and one rise falls the far side of midnight — and that day
Rem     answers None honestly rather than borrowing tomorrow's.
Rem
Rem   * EVERY ANGLE SUM IS WRAPPED BEFORE IT IS ANSWERED, and the moon
Rem     series wraps INSIDE too: 481268 degrees a century is the mean
Rem     motion, and by 2050 the raw sum is six figures. Wrap is floored
Rem     modulo, so dates before J2000 come out 0..360 like everything
Rem     else.
Rem
Rem   * FIELD NAMES CARRY THE PREFIX — EphRa, EphDec, EphAlt, EphAzim —
Rem     because an accessor loses every naming contest, and Alt, Dec
Rem     and Ra are words a program wants as locals.
Rem
Rem   * NO PARAMETER HERE IS CALLED e. math publishes E() for Euler's
Rem     number and names are case-insensitive, so the eccentric anomaly
Rem     is ea and the eccentricity ec, exactly as geo renamed its
Rem     excess for the same reason.
Rem
Rem Includes geo.shoddy for the observer — a GeoPt, its radian
Rem accessors, and the two wraps — and math.shoddy for Rad, Deg, Clamp
Rem and Hypot. It does not include julian: every date arrives as a
Rem Julian day NUMBER, which is julian's (and geo's) to make.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "geo.shoddy"
Include "math.shoddy"

Rem ---- the types -------------------------------------------------------

Rem A position on the celestial sphere: right ascension 0..360 and
Rem declination -90..90, both degrees, equinox of date. Fields are
Rem POSITIONAL; inserting one would shift every constructor in the
Rem tree silently.
Type EphSky
    EphRa  As Number
    EphDec As Number

Rem What an observer sees: altitude above the horizon and compass
Rem azimuth, both degrees.
Type EphView
    EphAlt  As Number
    EphAzim As Number

Rem ---- time, in the three shapes the formulas want ----------------------

Rem Days since J2000.0 at a UTC hour of a Julian date — the sun's
Rem clock, as in geo.
Def EphDays(jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    jd + hours / 24 - 2451545

Rem The same span in Julian centuries — the moon's and the planets'
Rem clock, because their published rates are per century.
Def EphT(jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    EphDays(jd, hours) / 36525

Rem Greenwich mean sidereal time, in degrees 0..360. The one formula
Rem that turns a clock time into a direction in the sky, and the whole
Rem reason a star rises four minutes earlier each night.
Def EphSidereal(jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    Wrap(280.46061837 + 360.98564736629 * EphDays(jd, hours), 360)

Rem ---- frames ------------------------------------------------------------

Rem The obliquity of the ecliptic — the tilt that makes seasons — to
Rem the same first-order rate geo's sun uses.
Def EphObliquity(n As Number) As Number
    23.439 - 0.0000004 * n

Rem Ecliptic longitude and latitude to right ascension and declination.
Rem THE ONE FRAME CHANGE IN THE MACHINE: the sun, the moon and every
Rem planet come out of their models in ecliptic coordinates and cross
Rem here exactly once.
Def EphSkyOf(lam As Number, beta As Number, n As Number) As EphSky
    EphSkyAt(Rad(lam), Rad(beta), Rad(EphObliquity(n)))

Def EphSkyAt(lr As Number, br As Number, er As Number) As EphSky
    EphSky(Wrap(Deg(Atn2(Sin(lr) * Cos(er) - Tan(br) * Sin(er), Cos(lr))), 360),
           Deg(Asin(Clamp(Sin(br) * Cos(er) + Cos(br) * Sin(er) * Sin(lr), -1, 1))))

Rem ---- the observer ------------------------------------------------------

Rem A sky position, as seen from a place at an instant. The hour angle
Rem is local sidereal time less right ascension, west positive — the
Rem same convention as geo's sun — and the azimuth formula is geo's:
Rem measured from south by the mathematics, turned into a compass
Rem bearing by the 180.
Def EphAltAz(p As GeoPt, sky As EphSky, jd As Number, hours As Number) As EphView
    EphViewAt(p, EphDec(sky), EphSidereal(jd, hours) + GeoLon(p) - EphRa(sky))

Def EphViewAt(p As GeoPt, dec As Number, ha As Number) As EphView
    EphView(Deg(Asin(Clamp(Sin(GeoLatR(p)) * Sin(Rad(dec)) + Cos(GeoLatR(p)) * Cos(Rad(dec)) * Cos(Rad(ha)), -1, 1))),
            GeoWrapBrg(Deg(Atn2(Sin(Rad(ha)), Cos(Rad(ha)) * Sin(GeoLatR(p)) - Tan(Rad(dec)) * Cos(GeoLatR(p)))) + 180))

Rem The angle between two sky positions. HAVERSINE, NOT THE LAW OF
Rem COSINES, for geo's stated reason: the cosine form throws away half
Rem its digits exactly where the interesting questions are — a moon
Rem two degrees from the sun, a planet near conjunction.
Def EphSeparation(a As EphSky, b As EphSky) As Number
    Let dDec = Rad(EphDec(b) - EphDec(a))
    Let dRa = Rad(EphRa(b) - EphRa(a))
    Let h = Sin(dDec / 2) ^ 2 + Cos(Rad(EphDec(a))) * Cos(Rad(EphDec(b))) * Sin(dRa / 2) ^ 2
    Deg(2 * Atn2(Sqr(Clamp(h, 0, 1)), Sqr(Clamp(1 - h, 0, 1))))

Rem ---- the sun -----------------------------------------------------------
Rem The same low-precision model as geo's sun words — mean longitude,
Rem mean anomaly, the equation of the centre to two terms — good to
Rem about a minute of arc. Kept private on geo's side and private
Rem here; the AGREEMENT of the two is asserted in tst/ephemeris.shoddy.

Def EphSunMean(n As Number) As Number
    Wrap(280.46 + 0.9856474 * n, 360)

Def EphSunAnom(n As Number) As Number
    Wrap(357.528 + 0.9856003 * n, 360)

Def EphSunLon(n As Number) As Number
    Wrap(EphSunMean(n) + 1.915 * Sin(Rad(EphSunAnom(n))) + 0.02 * Sin(Rad(2 * EphSunAnom(n))), 360)

Rem The sun as a sky position. For rise, set, noon and day length at a
Rem place, geo remains the machine; this word exists because the
Rem moon's phase and every planet's elongation are measured from it,
Rem and because EphAltAz of it answers where the sun stands in YOUR
Rem sky, which is a question geo does not spell.
Def EphSun(jd As Number, hours As Number) As EphSky
    EphSkyOf(EphSunLon(EphDays(jd, hours)), 0, EphDays(jd, hours))

Rem ---- the moon ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem The Astronomical Almanac's low-precision series: seven terms of
Rem longitude, four of latitude, five of parallax. About a third of a
Rem degree in longitude — GEOCENTRIC, see the header. Time is in
Rem Julian centuries because the published rates are.

Def EphMoonLon(t As Number) As Number
    Wrap(218.32 + 481267.881 * t
         + 6.29 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(135.0 + 477198.87 * t, 360)))
         - 1.27 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(259.3 - 413335.36 * t, 360)))
         + 0.66 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(235.7 + 890534.22 * t, 360)))
         + 0.21 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(269.9 + 954397.74 * t, 360)))
         - 0.19 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(357.5 + 35999.05 * t, 360)))
         - 0.11 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(186.5 + 966404.03 * t, 360))), 360)

Def EphMoonLat(t As Number) As Number
    (5.13 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(93.3 + 483202.02 * t, 360)))
     + 0.28 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(228.2 + 960400.89 * t, 360)))
     - 0.28 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(318.3 + 6003.15 * t, 360)))
     - 0.17 * Sin(Rad(Wrap(217.6 - 407332.21 * t, 360))))

Rem The equatorial horizontal parallax, in degrees — the moon's
Rem distance written as an angle, which is how the almanac carries it.
Def EphMoonPar(t As Number) As Number
    (0.9508
     + 0.0518 * Cos(Rad(Wrap(135.0 + 477198.87 * t, 360)))
     + 0.0095 * Cos(Rad(Wrap(259.3 - 413335.36 * t, 360)))
     + 0.0078 * Cos(Rad(Wrap(235.7 + 890534.22 * t, 360)))
     + 0.0028 * Cos(Rad(Wrap(269.9 + 954397.74 * t, 360))))

Def EphMoon(jd As Number, hours As Number) As EphSky
    EphSkyOf(EphMoonLon(EphT(jd, hours)), EphMoonLat(EphT(jd, hours)), EphDays(jd, hours))

Rem In metres: the Earth's equatorial radius over the sine of the
Rem parallax, which is what a parallax is.
Def EphMoonDistance(jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    6378140 / Sin(Rad(EphMoonPar(EphT(jd, hours))))

Rem ---- phase -------------------------------------------------------------

Def EphSynodic() As Number
    29.530588853

Rem The illuminated fraction, 0 at new through 1 at full — computed
Rem from the full angular separation of sun and moon, latitude
Rem included, which is what makes it honest during an eclipse.
Def EphMoonPhase(jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    (1 - Cos(Rad(EphSeparation(EphMoon(jd, hours), EphSun(jd, hours))))) / 2

Rem Days since new moon, 0 up to a synodic month. Phase says how much
Rem is lit; age says whether it is waxing or waning, which the
Rem fraction alone cannot.
Def EphMoonAge(jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    Wrap(EphMoonLon(EphT(jd, hours)) - EphSunLon(EphDays(jd, hours)), 360) / 360 * EphSynodic()

Rem ---- moonrise and moonset ----------------------------------------------

Rem The altitude of the moon's centre at the moment we call moonrise:
Rem the almanac's convention, which folds the average of the parallax
Rem this machine does not model against the refraction and
Rem semi-diameter it also does not model. A word, not a literal, so it
Rem can be seen and substituted — exactly GeoSunAltitude's shape.
Def EphMoonAltitude() As Number
    0.125

Rem The moon's altitude margin over the rise altitude, at a UTC hour —
Rem the function whose sign changes ARE moonrise and moonset.
Def EphMoonUp(p As GeoPt, jd As Number, hours As Number) As Number
    EphAlt(EphAltAz(p, EphMoon(jd, hours), jd, hours)) - EphMoonAltitude()

Rem UTC hours of moonrise, or None — see the header for both ways None
Rem is an ordinary answer. UNLIKE THE SUN there is no closed form: the
Rem moon moves its own width in an hour, so the day is walked hourly
Rem for a sign change and the crossing is then bisected. Twenty
Rem bisections is a third of a second of time, far inside the model.
Def EphMoonRise(p As GeoPt, jd As Number) As Option
    EphMoonCross(p, jd, 1)

Def EphMoonSet(p As GeoPt, jd As Number) As Option
    EphMoonCross(p, jd, -1)

Rem dir is 1 for a rise (margin crosses upward) and -1 for a set. The
Rem first crossing in the day is the one answered.
Def EphMoonCross(p As GeoPt, jd As Number, dir As Number) As Option
    EphCrossGo(p, jd, dir, 1, EphMoonUp(p, jd, 0))

Def EphCrossGo(p As GeoPt, jd As Number, dir As Number, k As Number, prev As Number) As Option
    If k > 24 Then
        None()
    Else
        Let now = EphMoonUp(p, jd, k)
        If dir * prev < 0 And dir * now >= 0 Then
            Some(EphCrossAt(p, jd, k - 1, k, dir, 20))
        Else
            EphCrossGo(p, jd, dir, k + 1, now)

Def EphCrossAt(p As GeoPt, jd As Number, lo As Number, hi As Number, dir As Number, k As Number) As Number
    Let mid = (lo + hi) / 2
    If k <= 0 Then
        mid
    Else
        If dir * EphMoonUp(p, jd, mid) < 0 Then
            EphCrossAt(p, jd, mid, hi, dir, k - 1)
        Else
            EphCrossAt(p, jd, lo, mid, dir, k - 1)

Rem ---- the planets -------------------------------------------------------
Rem The JPL approximate Keplerian elements, J2000 values plus rates per
Rem century, valid 1800..2050. Six numbers and six rates per body:
Rem a (AU), e, inclination, mean longitude, longitude of perihelion,
Rem longitude of the ascending node — the last four in degrees.
Rem
Rem THE CHOOSERS ARE WORDS, because Shoddy has no enum and a session
Rem should read EPHMARS, not 4. THE NUMBERING KEEPS THE EARTH'S SEAT:
Rem Mercury 1, Venus 2, Mars 4 — slot 3 is the Earth-Moon barycentre,
Rem which the geocentric subtraction below uses and a caller cannot
Rem ask for, because the Earth's position in the Earth's sky is where
Rem you are standing.

Def EphMercury() As Number
    1

Def EphVenus() As Number
    2

Def EphMars() As Number
    4

Def EphJupiter() As Number
    5

Def EphSaturn() As Number
    6

Rem { a, aDot, e, eDot, i, iDot, L, LDot, peri, periDot, node, nodeDot }
Def EphElems(k As Number) As List Of Number
    Select Case k
        Case 1
            { 0.38709927, 0.00000037, 0.20563593, 0.00001906, 7.00497902, -0.00594749,
              252.2503235, 149472.67411175, 77.45779628, 0.16047689, 48.33076593, -0.12534081 }
        Case 2
            { 0.72333566, 0.0000039, 0.00677672, -0.00004107, 3.39467605, -0.0007889,
              181.9790995, 58517.81538729, 131.60246718, 0.00268329, 76.67984255, -0.27769418 }
        Case 3
            { 1.00000261, 0.00000562, 0.01671123, -0.00004392, -0.00001531, -0.01294668,
              100.46457166, 35999.37244981, 102.93768193, 0.32327364, 0, 0 }
        Case 4
            { 1.52371034, 0.00001847, 0.0933941, 0.00007882, 1.84969142, -0.00813131,
              -4.55343205, 19140.30268499, -23.94362959, 0.44441088, 49.55953891, -0.29257343 }
        Case 5
            { 5.202887, -0.00011607, 0.04838624, -0.00013253, 1.30439695, -0.00183714,
              34.39644051, 3034.74612775, 14.72847983, 0.21252668, 100.47390909, 0.20469106 }
        Case Else
            { 9.53667594, -0.0012506, 0.05386179, -0.00050991, 2.48599187, 0.00193609,
              49.95424423, 1222.49362201, 92.59887831, -0.41897216, 113.66242448, -0.28867794 }

Rem Kepler's equation, M = E - e sin E, solved by Newton in degrees.
Rem ea IS THE ECCENTRIC ANOMALY AND NOT e — math publishes E() for
Rem Euler's number and names are case-insensitive. Eight iterations is
Rem far past convergence for every eccentricity in the table.
Def EphKepler(m As Number, ec As Number, ea As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k <= 0 Then
        ea
    Else
        EphKepler(m, ec, ea + (m - (ea - Deg(ec * Sin(Rad(ea))))) / (1 - ec * Cos(Rad(ea))), k - 1)

Rem A body's heliocentric ecliptic position at t centuries, in AU, as
Rem { x, y, z } — R4's one public vector spelling does not apply: this
Rem list never crosses the machine's surface.
Def EphHelio(k As Number, t As Number) As List Of Number
    EphHelioAt(EphElems(k), t)

Def EphHelioAt(el As List Of Number, t As Number) As List Of Number
    Let ec = Nth(el, 3) + Nth(el, 4) * t
    Let ml = Nth(el, 7) + Nth(el, 8) * t
    Let peri = Nth(el, 9) + Nth(el, 10) * t
    Let m = Wrap(ml - peri, 360)
    EphHelioOf(Nth(el, 1) + Nth(el, 2) * t, ec,
               Rad(Nth(el, 5) + Nth(el, 6) * t),
               Rad(EphKepler(m, ec, m + Deg(ec) * Sin(Rad(m)), 8)),
               Rad(peri - (Nth(el, 11) + Nth(el, 12) * t)),
               Rad(Nth(el, 11) + Nth(el, 12) * t))

Rem In-plane coordinates from the eccentric anomaly, then the standard
Rem rotation through the argument of perihelion, the inclination and
Rem the node, spelled once.
Def EphHelioOf(a As Number, ec As Number, inc As Number, ea As Number, w As Number, node As Number) As List Of Number
    Let xp = a * (Cos(ea) - ec)
    Let yp = a * Sqr(Clamp(1 - ec * ec, 0, 1)) * Sin(ea)
    { xp * (Cos(w) * Cos(node) - Sin(w) * Sin(node) * Cos(inc)) - yp * (Sin(w) * Cos(node) + Cos(w) * Sin(node) * Cos(inc)),
      xp * (Cos(w) * Sin(node) + Sin(w) * Cos(node) * Cos(inc)) + yp * (Cos(w) * Cos(node) * Cos(inc) - Sin(w) * Sin(node)),
      xp * Sin(w) * Sin(inc) + yp * Cos(w) * Sin(inc) }

Rem A planet as a sky position: its heliocentric place, less the
Rem Earth's, read as geocentric ecliptic and crossed into the
Rem equatorial frame. Light-time, aberration and nutation are below
Rem the stated accuracy and deliberately absent.
Def EphPlanet(which As Number, jd As Number, hours As Number) As EphSky
    If which = 3 Then
        Error("EPHPLANET: PLANET 3 IS THE EARTH, WHICH IS WHERE YOU ARE STANDING — GEO ANSWERS FOR IT")
    Else
        If which = 1 Or which = 2 Or which = 4 Or which = 5 Or which = 6 Then
            EphPlanetSky(which, jd, hours)
        Else
            Error("EPHPLANET: THERE IS NO PLANET '" & Str(which) & "' IN THE TABLE — USE EPHMERCURY THROUGH EPHSATURN")

Def EphPlanetSky(k As Number, jd As Number, hours As Number) As EphSky
    EphGeoSky(EphHelio(k, EphT(jd, hours)), EphHelio(3, EphT(jd, hours)), EphDays(jd, hours))

Def EphGeoSky(pv As List Of Number, ev As List Of Number, n As Number) As EphSky
    Let gx = Nth(pv, 1) - Nth(ev, 1)
    Let gy = Nth(pv, 2) - Nth(ev, 2)
    Let gz = Nth(pv, 3) - Nth(ev, 3)
    EphSkyOf(Deg(Atn2(gy, gx)), Deg(Atn2(gz, Hypot(gx, gy))), n)
